{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "נגף", :translit "nagaf", :gloss "[a] plague/striking/blow"} {:he "ג' במסורה", :translit "gimel b'mesorah", :gloss "three in the Masorah"} {:he "דין", :translit "din", :gloss "[this] one / the law [i.e., the instance at hand]"} {:he "ואידך", :translit "v'idech", :gloss "and the other [one]"} {:he "אך נגוף נגף הוא לפנינו", :translit "ach naguf nagaf hu lifaneinu", :gloss "but a smiting of smiting it is before us"} {:he "וירא ארם כי נגף לפני ישראל", :translit "vayyar aram ki nagaf lifnei yisrael", :gloss "and Aram saw that a smiting was before Israel"} {:he "אם שמוע תשמעו", :translit "im shamo'a tishme'u", :gloss "if hearing you will hear"} {:he "ינוסו לפניך", :translit "yanusu lifanecha", :gloss "they shall flee before you"} {:he "ואם לא תשמעו", :translit "v'im lo tishme'u", :gloss "and if you do not hear"} {:he "יתנך ה' נגף", :translit "yiteinchah Hashem nagaf", :gloss "the Lord will give you as a smiting"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["נגף" "נגף" "נגף"]}], :frame {:text "The root נגף carries the idea of divine striking or plague. At 1 Kings 20:29, Aram sees that נגף is before Israel — i.e., God strikes the enemy before Israel, as Deuteronomy 28:7 promises that enemies flee before the obedient. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the reverse for the disobedient: in Deuteronomy 28:25, the same root now falls on Israel themselves, so the identical word marks the reversal between blessing (Deuteronomy 28:1–2, 7) and curse (Deuteronomy 28:15, 25). The root appears at 1 Kings 20:29 in the form 'כי נגף לפני ישראל' and at 2 Chronicles 28:5 in the form 'אך נגוף נגף הוא לפנינו' (Pekah son of Remaliah kills 120,000 of Judah in one day).", :sources ["1 Kings 20:29" "2 Chronicles 28:5" "Deuteronomy 28:1" "Deuteronomy 28:2" "Deuteronomy 28:7" "Deuteronomy 28:15" "Deuteronomy 28:25"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["נגף" "נגף" "נגף"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "נגף", :refs ["Exodus